WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.220
Can two lines that cross show that memory has more than one system?

2
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.200
Watch one experiment.

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:15.560
In 1996, Knowlton, Mangels and Squire gave people a weather-prediction task.

4
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:19.993
Cue cards appeared, and the person guessed one of two outcomes.

5
00:00:20.973 --> 00:00:23.313
Each card predicted it only with some probability.

6
00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:29.307
No single trial is decisive, so the task is learned gradually, from feedback.

7
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:34.110
Afterwards, a questionnaire asked about the training sessions themselves.

8
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:40.590
Three groups took part: controls, amnesic patients, and patients with Parkinson's

9
00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.550
disease, whose striatum loses dopamine input.

10
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:49.330
Over the first fifty trials, controls and amnesic patients learned the

11
00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:50.450
classification together.

12
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.450
The Parkinson's patients failed to learn it.

13
00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:58.033
Take the simplest difficulty model: one common capacity.

14
00:00:58.993 --> 00:01:01.673
Suppose the questionnaire is simply the harder task.

15
00:01:02.473 --> 00:01:04.813
The Parkinson's patients failed the easier one.

16
00:01:05.613 --> 00:01:07.013
How should they do on the questionnaire?

17
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:15.267
On that model, they should fail it too.

18
00:01:16.207 --> 00:01:17.707
Instead, they remembered the training.

19
00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:22.747
The amnesic patients had severely impaired memory for the training.

20
00:01:23.587 --> 00:01:24.827
Yet they learned the classification.

21
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.490
So no single order of difficulty fits both groups.

22
00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:34.190
Each group does far worse on the task the other does well, and the lines cross.

23
00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:39.670
These are group averages, so the crossing argues against the simplest difficulty

24
00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:43.470
account rather than ruling out every single-capacity one.

25
00:01:44.433 --> 00:01:47.753
Now, a trap. Some answers are right by guessing.

26
00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:53.467
In this illustrative design, guessing alone gets half right on the two-choice task.

27
00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.487
On a four-choice questionnaire, it gets a quarter.

28
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:03.667
So measure each score up from that baseline, as a share of the controls' gain above

29
00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:04.667
it.

30
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.900
An invented bar that looks like half of normal shrinks to about a quarter.

31
00:02:11.483 --> 00:02:13.923
The correction changes how big a loss looks.

32
00:02:14.763 --> 00:02:18.403
As long as controls beat chance, it cannot change which group is higher.

33
00:02:19.243 --> 00:02:20.243
So the crossing stays.

34
00:02:21.317 --> 00:02:24.537
Parkinson's disease damages more than the striatum.

35
00:02:25.397 --> 00:02:29.817
So this crossing supports separable learning processes, without pinning each to one

36
00:02:29.817 --> 00:02:30.817
structure.

37
00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.697
Now a second crossing, in three patients.

38
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.250
A signal was paired with an aversive stimulus.

39
00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:39.410
Did a sweat response appear?

40
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:41.710
Could the patient report the pairing?

41
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.320
A patient with damage to both amygdalae could report the pairing, but showed no

42
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:48.320
conditioned response.

43
00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:52.733
A patient with damage to both hippocampi showed the reverse.

44
00:02:53.817 --> 00:02:57.137
Predict the third patient, with damage to both structures.

45
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.620
That patient learned neither.

46
00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:13.073
Two crossings. In these studies, new memory that could be reported and memory shown

47
00:03:13.073 --> 00:03:15.213
in performance depended on different structures.
